          "content": "Lines and Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.12,
            0.836,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": "6. Now mark any four points on your paper so that no three of them are on one line. Label them A, B, C, D. Draw all possible lines going through pairs of these points. How many lines do you get? Name them. How many angles can you name using A, B, C, D? Write them all down, and mark each of them with a curve as in Fig. 2.9."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.259,
            0.438,
            0.287
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.6 Comparing Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.3,
            0.835,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": "Look at these animals opening their mouths. Do you see any angles here? If yes, mark the arms and vertex of each one. Some mouths are open wider than others; the more the turning of the jaws, the larger the angle! Can you arrange the angles in this picture from smallest to largest?"
        },
        {
